th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028+v3-2026-11-02+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+type-mix-retire-20261112+type-mix-topup-20261112+type-mix-retire2-20261112+fill-20261112+fill2-20261112","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/universality-of-the-uniform.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Probability-Theory-for-EOR/universality-of-the-uniform.html","questions":[{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"How can a discrete distribution be simulated using the unit interval?","options":["Make every piece the same size regardless of probability","Divide the unit interval into probability-sized pieces","Round the uniform draw $U$ to the nearest possible outcome value","Divide the unit interval into pieces sized by the outcome values"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c9a886c1dddf93e485570a34","time_limit":25},{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $F$ be a CDF with quantile function $Q$ and let $U\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$. Which is an example of the universality idea?","options":["Making a uniform by applying $Q$ to $X\\sim F$","Making $X\\sim F$ as $F(U)$, $U$ uniform","Making $X\\sim F$ as $Q(U)$, $U$ uniform","Making $X\\sim F$ by rescaling $U$ linearly"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1eb87f54556652068b28ba1c","time_limit":35},{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are generalized inverses important for discrete targets?","options":["Discrete CDFs have jumps and may lack ordinary inverses.","Discrete CDFs are strictly increasing, so inverses are unique.","Discrete targets need a uniform input that is itself discrete.","Generalized inverses turn a discrete target into a continuous one."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f84ee6e390f00c4143bd623","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_004_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The theorem builds $X=Q(U)$ with $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le x)=F(x)$ for a function $F\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to[0,1]$. Which property of $F$ is not required for this?","options":["Continuity of $F$","Right-continuity of $F$","$F$ is nondecreasing","$F(x)\\to0$ as $x\\to-\\infty$ and $F(x)\\to1$ as $x\\to\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f5fe13419c70654fd7fbc79c","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_006_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $U\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ and $F(x)=1-\\mathrm{e}^{-2x}$ for $x\\ge0$, $F(x)=0$ for $x<0$. Which variable has CDF $F$ by the quantile construction?","options":["$-\\tfrac12\\log(1-U)$","$-2\\log(1-U)$","$1-\\mathrm{e}^{-2U}$","$\\tfrac12\\log(1-U)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ff5b2516248e9d22ebaa09a8","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_011_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ have CDF $F$. Which statement about $F(X)$ is correct?","options":["$F(X)\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ whenever $F$ is continuous","$F(X)\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ for every CDF $F$, including ones with jumps","$F(X)\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ only if $F$ is strictly increasing","$F(X)\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ only if $X$ is itself uniform"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c6f289d4d3fd2d2fd910f029","time_limit":35},{"id":"pt_batch9_universality_of_the_uniform_017_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $F$ be a CDF, $Q(u)=\\inf\\{x\\colon F(x)\\ge u\\}$ and $U\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$. Which step shows that $X=Q(U)$ has CDF $F$?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(Q(U)\\le x)=\\mathbb{P}(U\\le F(x))=F(x)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Q(U)\\le x)=\\mathbb{P}(U\\ge F(x))=1-F(x)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Q(U)\\le x)=\\mathbb{P}(U\\le Q(x))=Q(x)$","$\\mathbb{P}(F(U)\\le x)=F(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c2f7a141426c9aa223fc5303","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_probability_theory_for_eor_universality_of_the_uniform_71e40f15_fill20261112_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $U\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$, and let $F(x)=0$ for $x<0$, $F(x)=\\frac{1+x}{2}$ for $0\\le x<1$ and $F(x)=1$ for $x\\ge1$. Which random variable has CDF $F$?","options":["$\\max(0,\\,2U-1)$","$2U-1$","$\\frac{1+U}{2}$","$U$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6e7137860d018a3e82463f61","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_probability_theory_for_eor_universality_of_the_uniform_71e40f15_fill20261112_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A random variable $X$ has density $f_X(x)=2x$ for $0<x<1$ and $f_X(x)=0$ otherwise. What is the distribution of $Y=X^2$?","options":["$Y$ has density $2y$ for $0<y<1$, the same as $X$","$Y$ has density $\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}$ for $0<y<1$","$Y\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$","$Y$ has density $4y^3$ for $0<y<1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7b8abad0d788217d3f2c0b5a","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_probability_theory_for_eor_universality_of_the_uniform_71e40f15_fill20261112_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $U\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$, and let $F(x)=0$ for $x<0$, $F(x)=x^3$ for $0\\le x\\le1$ and $F(x)=1$ for $x>1$. The quantile construction gives $X=U^{1/3}$, which has CDF $F$. Does $W=(1-U)^{1/3}$ also have CDF $F$?","options":["Yes, because $1-U$ is also uniform on $(0,1)$","No, $W$ has CDF $1-F$, because $U$ was replaced by $1-U$","Yes, but only because $F$ is continuous; with jumps in $F$ it would fail","No, because only $Q(U)$, not $Q(1-U)$, has CDF $F$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c6b969a8a8b2057627636d04","time_limit":55}]}}